// Regression for a correctness bug found while fixing issues #350/#388: a
// catch clause's own binding (`catch (e) { ... }`) is always a FRESH
// declaration, never an assignment to an existing captured local -- but on
// the JVM backend its slot index can be one `capturedSlots` already marks
// captured, from an earlier, DIFFERENT local in the same try body whose
// closure escapes the try (here, `c`, appended to `fns` and called after
// the catch runs). Before this fix, `e`'s own SET_LOCAL went through the
// generic captured-slot raw-or-cell check (emitCapturedStore), which saw
// that still-live cell and wrote `e`'s value INTO it -- silently
// corrupting `c`'s own captured value instead of binding `e`. This program
// used to print "boom" correctly, then crash on `fns[0]()` with a type
// error, because `x`'s cell held "boom" instead of 41 by the time `c` ran.

var fns = [];
var doThrow = true;
try {
    var x = 41;
    fun c() { return x + 1; }
    fns.append(c);
    // Gated behind a flag, not an unconditional throw: an unconditional
    // throw here would make the try body's own normal-completion close
    // structurally unreachable, which the capture corpus keeps at zero
    // (test_backend_capture.cpp's own CaptureAnalysisTest.
    // NoAssertionFailureOnExamples measures this across every example) —
    // see test/translation-probes/60_throw_ends_try_binding.lox's own
    // header comment for the same pattern.
    if (doThrow) { throw "boom"; }
} catch (e) { print e; }
print fns[0]();

// CHECK: boom
// CHECK: 42
